    "created_at": "2021-06-06T18:38:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-06-06T18:38:16Z",
    "body": "In his response to the Chrome issue linked in the description, @tkent-google pointed to the [focus fixup rule](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#focus-fixup-rule) and [unfocusing steps](#unfocusing-steps) to show that the focusing steps and focus update steps are called and therefore `blur` and `change` events are triggered. Both then and now, I understand that the unfocusing steps short circuit before performing any meaningful work (then at [steps 5 and 6](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/commit-snapshots/a6e54621c0491ef633b0f3c4bd1f71fe72a722d8/#unfocusing-steps), now at [step 5](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#unfocusing-steps)), so we can concentrate solely on the **focus fixup rule**.\r\n\r\nThe focus fixup rule currently says, unchanged from then:\r\n\r\n> **Focus fixup rule**: When the designated [focused area of the document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#focused-area-of-the-document) is removed from that [`Document`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#document) in some way (e.g. it stops being a [focusable area](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#focusable-area), it is removed from the DOM, it becomes [expressly inert](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#expressly-inert), etc.), designate the [`Document`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#document)'s [viewport](https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport) to be the new [focused area of the document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#focused-area-of-the-document).\r\n\r\nDoes \"designate _focusable area x_ to be the new focused area of the document\" imply that the focusing steps should be run? It's a little ambiguous. If it did, I believe the [focus update steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#focus-update-steps) would become recursive from step 4.1:\r\n\r\n> If _entry_ is a [focusable area](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#focusable-area): designate _entry_ as the [focused area of the document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#focused-area-of-the-document).\r\n\r\nThe [focusing steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#focusing-steps) themselves state that they should be run \"whenever the user attempts to move the focus\", which wouldn't cover an element being removed from the document.\r\n\r\nTo address this as well as other ambiguity I perceive to exist in the rule, last week I filed https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/6729. I only came across this issue today while doing further research on the topic, but depending on how implementers view these points, it may be that your preferred behaviour is already the more accepted reading of the current spec.",
